  "id": "foucault-coriolis-pendulum-fourth-science",
  "name": "Foucault pendulum: small-angle gyroscopic precession",
  "category": "physics",
  "description": "Two horizontal small-angle displacement components with restoring acceleration and velocity-coupled Coriolis terms. Omega_vertical is the signed vertical rotation frequency; restoring frequency omega0. Centrifugal and vertical motion terms are omitted in the source approximation. Default normalized omega0=1/s,Omega_vertical=0.05/s,x0=1m,all other states0; end60 RK4step0.02. Rotation is deliberately accelerated for a visible illustration, not an Earth latitude or calibrated apparatus. Small angle requires displacement much smaller than pendulum length; g/L=omega0^2 alone does not fix g or L. Energy per unit mass is conserved. Reverse rotation reverses precession; zero rotation recovers planar harmonic motion. Original mathematical transcription/derivation; parameters are illustrative, not empirical calibration. Signed net-rate flows represent derivatives, not nonnegative physical transfers. No clamps or hidden resets.",
  "source": "https://theory.physics.manchester.ac.uk/~mikeb/lecture/pc167/noninertial/foucault.html",
  "reference": "Mike Birse, PC1672 Advanced dynamics section2.5 Foucault pendulum, Manchester (17 May2000): coupled horizontal equations and frequency roots. Original first-order transcription; not copied lecture text/figures.",
